From: Canaan K230 Linux SDK
Subject: [PATCH] live555: fix RTP-over-TCP stalls, missing close notification, IPv6 compare

Three fixes carried by the rt-smart tree's vendored live555 copy
(canmv_k230_dpu/src/rtsmart/mpp/middleware/src/live555), rebased here onto
2021.05.03. They are platform-neutral; the rt-smart/lwIP workarounds that
live next to them (SO_REUSEPORT, SO_LINGER, the disabled IPv6 listener, the
debug printfs) are deliberately NOT carried over.

1. liveMedia/RTPInterface.cpp, liveMedia/include/RTPInterface.hh
   RTP-over-TCP (rtsp_transport=tcp, which is what most players pick when
   UDP is blocked) used to be sent as two writes: the 4-byte interleaved
   header, then the packet.  If the second write only partly fit in the
   socket buffer, live555 switched the socket to blocking mode and retried
   with a 500ms timeout.

   That blocks the single-threaded event loop.  Nothing else is served for
   up to half a second -- including reading this very socket, which is where
   the receiver's RTCP "RR"s and the window updates arrive, so the stall
   feeds itself.  With several clients, or one client on a slow link, the
   timeout eventually fires and live555 drops the connection: the player
   freezes and then disconnects, typically a few seconds after an I-frame.

   Now the header and payload are copied into one buffer and sent as a
   single write, so a frame that does not fit can simply be dropped (which
   is what RTP over UDP does anyway, and the receiver handles).  Only a
   genuinely partial write leaves a tail, and that tail is finished from a
   SOCKET_WRITABLE callback rather than by blocking.  Reads stay enabled
   throughout.

2. liveMedia/RTSPClient.cpp, liveMedia/include/RTSPClient.hh
   An RTSP client streaming over TCP was never told when the server closed
   the connection.  live555 reports a dead socket by failing whatever
   request is awaiting a response, but after "PLAY" there is no such
   request, so the event was dropped on the floor: the client sat in its
   event loop forever with no data and no error.

   Adds a virtual handleTCPConnectionClosed() hook, posted as a zero-delay
   task (the detection happens inside the socket descriptor's destructor,
   so the subclass must not tear things down re-entrantly).  The task is
   unscheduled in ~RTSPClient so it cannot outlive the client.

   package/librtsp_client already overrides this hook -- without this patch
   that override compiles but is never called, and KdRtspClient never
   reports a disconnect.

3. groupsock/NetAddress.cpp
   IPv6 addresses were compared with "==" on "s6_addr", which is a
   16-element array: that compares the addresses of two different arrays
   and so is always false.  Two equal IPv6 addresses never compared equal.
   Replaced with an element-wise comparison.

diff -urN /tmp/l_a/groupsock/NetAddress.cpp /tmp/l_b/groupsock/NetAddress.cpp
--- a/groupsock/NetAddress.cpp	2026-11-21 08:02:27.132435977 +0800
+++ b/groupsock/NetAddress.cpp	2026-11-21 08:03:35.409667005 +0800
@@ -160,7 +160,15 @@
       return ((struct sockaddr_in const&)left).sin_addr.s_addr == ((struct sockaddr_in const&)right).sin_addr.s_addr;
     }
     case AF_INET6: {
-      return ((struct sockaddr_in6 const&)left).sin6_addr.s6_addr == ((struct sockaddr_in6 const&)right).sin6_addr.s6_addr;
+      // Local patch; reapply when upgrading live555.
+      // "s6_addr" is a 16-byte array, so comparing the two with "==" compares
+      // the addresses of two different arrays and is therefore always false.
+      u_int8_t const* leftAddr = ((struct sockaddr_in6 const&)left).sin6_addr.s6_addr;
+      u_int8_t const* rightAddr = ((struct sockaddr_in6 const&)right).sin6_addr.s6_addr;
+      for (unsigned i = 0; i < 16; ++i) {
+	if (leftAddr[i] != rightAddr[i]) return False;
+      }
+      return True;
     }
     default: {
       return False;
